  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday morning through next Thursday)\nIssued at 243 PM EDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nThe long-term pattern is pretty straightforward, with an upper-\nlevel ridge building over the area through the extended period. \nForecast guidance and ML products showcase plentiful moisture \nadvection from the Gulf along the western periphery of the Bermuda\nHigh, along with enough daily instability to support diurnally \ndriven thunderstorm chances each day through next week. The \ncombination of precipitable water values ranging from 1.5-1.9 \ninches and a lack of well established steering could lead to some \nlocalized flash flooding with any more robust convective \ndevelopments. However, severe potential remains limited to the \ndaily heating influences, given little deep-layer wind shear or \npropagating shortwaves to aid in the development of more enhanced \nconvection. That being said, there is enough daily instability to \nsupport isolated strong to severe storms with gusty winds being \nthe primary threat. As the ridge builds over our area, \nprecipitation chances do look to decrease somewhat compared to \nrecent days. \n\nHigh temperatures will gradually climb into the low to mid-90s \nthrough the period. Combined with dewpoints in the low 70s, \nconditions will push heat indices into the triple digits by the \nsecond half of next week. At that point, we will need to monitor the \nheat index as values approach the 105-degree mark for the possible \nissuance of a Heat Advisory.",
